  "final_text": "Wrote `impact.txt` at the repo root with the two definitions that use `_check_nested_chain`:\n\n```\nsrc/click/core.py#Group.add_command\nsrc/click/core.py#CommandCollection.get_command\n```\n\nHow I got there:\n- `who` on `src/click/core.py#_check_nested_chain` reported 4 backlinks, all in `src/click/core.py`, with complete coverage over the crawled revision (166 files: src 17, tests 47, examples 14, docs 1; no unresolved reference matching the name).\n- Two of those backlinks are the enclosing class pages (`Group` at core.py:1649, `CommandCollection` at core.py:2119) for the same uses in their methods \u2014 the actual call sites are `core.py:1788` (`Group.add_command`) and `core.py:2162` (`CommandCollection.get_command`). Per the task's rule I listed the methods, not the classes; nothing appears in a class body itself.\n- I read the full bodies of `Group` and `CommandCollection` to confirm there is no additional use credited to the class pages, and checked the tests: no test references the name. `tests/test_chain.py` and `tests/test_commands.py` (the chain/group-registration tests) contain no use, and cross-file references from tests are tracked by the index (e.g. `tests/test_shell_completion.py#test_chained` backlinks `core.Group`), so an imported use in tests would have shown up.\n\nOne caveat worth stating: the index records name references, not attribute accesses (e.g. a hypothetical `click.core._check_nested_chain(...)` in a test would be invisible to it, as `self.add_command` calls are). I read the two most likely test files directly and found nothing, but I could not text-search the whole `tests/` tree with the tools available.\n\nNo test command was named in the task, so I ran nothing; no other file was changed.",
